+# Next.js Frontend — Migration Plan
+
+This directory is a **parallel** Next.js UI for the Zambia Treasury Insight FastAPI backend.
+The existing vanilla-JS dashboard at `static/index.html` continues to be served by FastAPI
+unchanged. This Next.js app can be deployed separately (e.g. on Vercel) and pointed at the
+backend via the `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` env var.
+
+## Why migrate?
+
+The existing 487-line `static/index.html` works but has limitations:
+- No component reuse — every tab duplicates layout and chart-setup boilerplate.
+- No type safety — API contract changes silently break the UI.
+- No client-side caching — every tab refetches everything.
+- Hard to test — the JS is inline, not module-based.
+
+A Next.js + TypeScript + React Query + Plotly React frontend fixes all four.
+
+## What's in this scaffold
+
+```
+frontend/
+├── package.json          # Next 16, React 19, react-plotly.js, @tanstack/react-query, Tailwind 4
+├── tsconfig.json
+├── next.config.mjs
+├── app/
+│   ├── layout.tsx        # Root layout with the 8-tab sidebar
+│   ├── globals.css       # Tailwind 4 + CSS variables for light/dark theme
+│   └── page.tsx          # Overview tab (working: fetches /api/status + /api/overview)
+├── lib/
+│   └── api.ts            # Typed API client with all 17 endpoints + Basic Auth helper
+└── MIGRATION.md          # This file
+```
+
+## Migration order
+
+The Overview tab is the reference implementation. Build the remaining 7 tabs in this order:
+
+1. **`/data`** — Data & quality tab. Uses `api.status()`. Renders the per-series table,
+   ingest log, problems list, and a "Refresh now" button that calls `api.refresh()`.
+   Simplest tab — good place to nail down the shared `QueryClientProvider` and
+   `useQuery` patterns.
+
+2. **`/audit`** — Audit log tab. Uses `api.audit()`. A simple table with sorting.
+   Good first tab for `@tanstack/react-table`.
+
+3. **`/model`** — Model documentation tab. Uses `api.modelDocs()`. Renders the spec,
+   drivers table, backtest summary, data sources, and a "Download PDF" button that
+   links to `api.modelDocsPdfUrl()`. Mostly static rendering.
+
+4. **`/forecast`** — Forecast tab. Uses `api.forecast()` + `api.backtest()`. The first
+   chart-heavy tab. Build the `ForecastFanChart` component here using react-plotly.js —
+   it's reused on `/explain` and `/history`.
+
+5. **`/explain`** — Driver attribution tab. Uses `api.explain()` + `api.events()` +
+   `api.regimes()`. Builds the `USZMWChartWithRegimes` component (USD/ZMW line +
+   regime bands + event markers). The decomposition bar chart is unique to this tab.
+
+6. **`/history`** — History tab. Uses `api.history()`. Renders the swing-episode list
+   with per-episode attribution. Reuses the chart components from `/explain`.
+
+7. **`/scenario`** — Scenario tab. Uses `api.scenarioPresets()` + `api.scenario()` (POST).
+   The only tab that does a POST — good place to nail down the mutation patterns
+   (invalidate the audit query after a scenario post so the audit log updates).
+
+## Shared components to build
+
+- `<QueryProvider>` — wraps the app in `QueryClientProvider`. Put in `app/providers.tsx`.
+- `<USZMWChart>` — Plotly line chart of USD/ZMW. Props: `data: SeriesData`, optional
+  `regimes: Regime[]`, optional `events: RegimeEvent[]`.
+- `<RegimeBands>` — Plotly shape layer drawing vertical bands at regime breakpoints.
+- `<EventMarkers>` — Plotly annotation layer for events.
+- `<DecompositionBar>` — Plotly bar chart of driver contributions (positive/negative).
+- `<ForecastFan>` — Plotly line + filled-interval trace for the forecast.
+- `<BacktestTable>` — sticky-header table of backtest metrics with metric-definition tooltips.
+- `<DataModeBanner>` — the demo/mixed/live/empty banner with notes.
+- `<AuthDialog>` — modal that prompts for Basic Auth credentials when `/api/security`
+  reports `auth_enabled: true`.
+
+## Running locally
+
+```bash
+cd frontend
+npm install
+
+# Develop against the live Render backend (default):
+npm run dev
+# Open http://localhost:3000
+
+# Or develop against a local FastAPI instance:
+NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8000 npm run dev
+```
+
+## Deploying (separate from the FastAPI backend)
+
+The cleanest deployment is **Vercel** (Next.js's native host):
+
+1. Push the `frontend/` directory to a separate GitHub repo (or a `frontend/` subdir
+   of the main repo).
+2. Import into Vercel — it auto-detects Next.js.
+3. Set `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://zambia-treasury-insight.onrender.com` in the Vercel
+   env vars.
+4. Deploy.
+
+Alternatively, deploy on Render as a separate Static Site:
+- Build command: `cd frontend && npm install && npm run build`
+- Publish directory: `frontend/.next/static` (Render serves the static export).
+- Or use `next start` on a Render Web Service for the full SSR version.
+
+## Authentication
+
+If the backend has `ZTI_PASSWORD` set, the frontend prompts for Basic Auth credentials
+on first load. Credentials are stored in `localStorage` as a base64-encoded
+`user:password` string and attached to every API request via the `Authorization`
+header.
+
+This is NOT secure for production with sensitive data — Basic Auth over HTTPS is
+acceptable for this app's threat model (public market data + an operator password
+to prevent anon data uploads), but for a real bank deployment use a proper
+session/OAuth layer.
+
+## What's NOT in this scaffold (yet)
+
+- The actual chart components (`USZMWChart`, `DecompositionBar`, etc.) — these are
+  the bulk of the remaining work. Each is ~50-100 lines of react-plotly.js.
+- The `/explain`, `/history`, `/forecast`, `/scenario`, `/model`, `/audit`, `/data`
+  route files. Each is ~100-200 lines of TSX.
+- The `QueryClientProvider` setup in `app/providers.tsx`.
+- Light/dark theme toggle (the CSS variables are in place; the toggle button isn't).
+- Auth dialog component.
+- Mobile responsive layout (the sidebar should collapse on narrow screens).
+
+Estimated total work to finish: ~1,500-2,000 lines of TSX + ~500 lines of chart
+components. A focused developer can complete it in 2-3 days.
+
+## When to switch over
+
+The FastAPI backend continues to serve `static/index.html` at `/` until you're ready
+to switch. To switch:
+
+1. Complete the 7 remaining tabs in this scaffold.
+2. Deploy the Next.js app to Vercel (or wherever).
+3. Update the FastAPI `app/main.py` `index()` route to redirect `/` to the Next.js URL,
+   OR remove the `index()` route entirely and serve only the API from FastAPI.
+4. Keep `static/index.html` in the repo as a fallback (it's still useful for offline
+   demos and for users without JS).
+
+The migration is reversible — if the Next.js app has issues, point `/` back at
+`static/index.html` and the FastAPI serves the old UI again.
